JS: I don't remember, I remember Saturdays. Only, I don't know how they knew it was Saturday, but people would talk about what food they would usually have on a Shabbat at home. And just to make us more hungry, I guess. [LAUGHING] And they told stories of what they used to do. And I don't know what we did. I mean, just surviving, going from here to go pee over there and, [GESTURING] and to stay warm and to be near the fire when they were cooking something, it was really hard, and I honestly don't remember that much of it. I remember that one time, my father was always, you know, what he saw, he looked and he saw something on a string and, pulled it up, [GESTURING] pulled it up, and it was a bag that one of the families had their grandmother there, and she carried the family money, and that was the money. But I'll just, go back a little bit. And he. So it wasn't, the food wasn't bought as a group. Everybody kind of like, had their own guy who came, and my father had run out of money, and he asked somebody in our group to lend him some money, and they refused. You know, it's it was hard survival. So he found this. And he let them wait a little bit and then he took it to them. But I think they still didn't lend him any money. But then, I don't know, he got it somehow differently, I don't know. But so it was hard. So you had to strive for survival from the Nazis, from, you know, the peasants had to come and help you because they could use the few cents they got from you. And then your fellow, so [?Martha?] was different. That's why she stood out, you know, and there were different people, but some were there to survive, and so were we. We were all like that, I guess.

INT: Were you frightened?

JS: I was never really frightened. I was frightened once, but I was just coming to that part. So we were running out of food, and it was decided that a group will go down to the nearest village and knock on doors and get crust of bread. These villages were poor. So my mother, it was mostly women going down. And one guy went with them because only Jewish men were circumcised at that time. And so they could tell a man just to drop your pants and they knew right away. So my mother was protecting my father. She went down and they had a signal that they'll whistle or something when there's danger, and they'll gather and they'll all come back. [GESTURING] So they came back. Except, not my mother. I, honestly I get shivers when I talk about this, not my mother. So of course I'm scared beyond scared. And now my father goes down to look for my mother. And it's meal time. So it was, I guess it was one of those meals that somebody, there was a horse, a wounded horse, and they managed to kill him and they were cooking it. And so that was horse for lunch. And one lady was doling out the food and she. So I speak Hungarian and Slovak because those were the languages there, and some German. And she said to me, "You're a good girl. You'll be satisfied with this too." And she gives me a bone, and I'm eight years old and I'm thinking, "This is not going to do well for me." So I'm scared. I'm more scared, and my parents aren't there. But they come back and we're okay. And, you know, all is well as well as can be. And so we lived through the winter, and I don't know how we got through it. And, and we can hear. So the Russian army, the Red Army is coming from the east. We're in the central Slovakia. So it's starting to liberate almost where our home was. Humenné. Because that was right, almost on the border. [GESTURING] And we can hear, the Russians had a very, I don't know what it looked like, a gun. I mean, it was a. Like it wasn't a gun, handgun. It was a huge gun called Katyusha for a girl's name. And you could hear it from eastern Slovakia. It was that huge. And, we could hear the Katyushas, but we were still in the mountains where people around us are. God knows they're Nazis. they're, you know, they're murderers, they're, we heard that one valley of Jewish people there, was actually all killed. So, you know, my father stood guard every night. They had to stand guard. My father never held a gun in his hands. But, you know, they all had to take turns. So it's still a hard times. And then my parents must have decided that I was getting weak. And, they want to strengthen me up. So to take me down to a village and let mother and me stay in the village with a villager, that I don't know how they connected with him. So my mother and I go out. Go down, I don't know how she found the place. I don't remember going with anybody else because actually the [?Teller?] family went down as well. So we come down and it was kind of end of winter, sort of like it was, but still hard in the mountains. And we come down to where there are, the fields are starting, the farmer's fields, and it was so hard to walk on because they were still frozen and very uneven. And I'm stumbling. And the guy who was, that they agreed would would do this for us. He is there to meet us. His name is I'll cry when I call his name. [CRYING] And [?Joseph Shebo?] comes and meets my mother and he sees that I'm stumbling. He puts me on his back. [?Joseph Shebo?] is, he has one arm only. He carries me to his house on his one arm and we come to his house. House is... The house was probably no bigger than like this part here. [GESTURING] He has a wife and two girls or two children, a girl and a boy. And it's warm. It's a small place and he gives us their beds. We have lice and he puts, they heat up, there's no heating. They heat it up bricks to warm up the bed. And mother and I go to bed. They give us something to eat and we have a good sleep. We wake up in the morning. The village, which probably had like maybe 20 houses, is encircled by Germans with, I think it could have been Slovaks. Nazis with rifles. The word is out. [GESTURING] Anybody who's harboring a Jew. We burn your house down. We take you so let out the Jews. He, of course, didn't do that. So there was a plan that I wasn't aware of at that moment, that we go to another place that was bigger and that had a barn. And in the barn there was a lot of hay, and in the hay there was a crate, a wooden crate that was prepared. I don't know for what, but it was there for us. The [?Teller?] family went to that farmer and Now he dresses us up. So I think we just had our old clothes. But the village women had these wide skirts and they had that tight My mother and me. [GESTURING] Now the Nazis are there with the guns pointing at us, and he's walking with two Jewish people through the village to that other farmer. I don't know that other's name. And there we go to the to the barn. And you go through the straw hay. I don't know, it was it was dry and you kind of like fall into this box. The box was like about the size of the dining room table. And Mr. And Mrs. [?Teller?] are there with the two boys that I love, you know. And so my mother and I slip in there and we were there. And you just have to crouch. There was nothing. There was no food there. We were just sitting there and then the owner of that house, so [?Shebo?] goes, you know, and the owner of that farm comes into the barn and calls to us and says, "You have to get out because they'll burn me down if they find you." So we didn't go. I guess they just refused and couldn't do anything. So then we hear the Germans go, the Nazis go from door to door, and they came to this farm. And we know that they're there because they're in the pigsty and the pigs are squealing. And they came to the doorway, that is to the barn. And we hear their voices. And then they leave. Now if in that moment I would have sneezed, it would have been the end of us. If the. If the rifle guys had seen mother and me looking maybe a little different from the village women, that would be the end of us. I just can't believe, you know, that was. That was just horrible. But we survived. They left with, they they did gather a few Jews. I don't know who they were. They left with them. That night, all clear, my mother and I go back to the mountain. That was our, you know, R&R. But we survived. And [?Joseph Shebo?] is the hero of this story. I mean, he is. And I have to get busy and and get in touch with Yad Vashem and have him righteous Gentile. I had my, so I have cousins in Slovakia, and I had at that time, and I got in touch with her and she she searched for him and she couldn't find him anymore. I think those villages, you know, the young people are gone. They the old ones died. So she couldn't find anybody. She managed to find the people who saved her, and she had them named at Yad Vashem. But so we now we get back in the mountains and we still are not liberated, but the front is moving closer to us. And then, there was, I guess, discussion that I wasn't privy to. I wouldn't have understood that. Now we if we go through the territory that's still considered Nazi occupied, which is very dangerous to go through, and we could be caught at any moment. And it's winter still like it's spring, but it's winter in the mountains. And, we could come to a mountain, if we crossed that mountain. We'll be on the liberated side. So it was decided by our group that we were going. [WIPING NOSE] And all along there was some food. We had some food stored that in case we need it later, because, like, you know, we never know. And that night, everybody knew we were leaving. And there's the food. So they tried to kind of, like, warm it up, cook it up, make a fire. And nobody wanted to eat. We couldn't eat. It was just you were too nervous to eat. So I remember I guess it must have been left behind. So now we start the trek through the snow, and we have to be very quiet and careful. And we're walking through the snow, through different valleys and whatever. And we get to a town. I don't know what the town is called. It's at the foot of the mountain called And there when we arrive, oh and then Mrs. [?Teller?] by now is so unwell that they bring her down on a blanket. Four people carrying her down. She must have been a young woman. She had teenage boys. And, whoever was with our group said, "We can't leave now because the storms are terrible on these mountains and you can't survive it." So we wait till that morning and we take off in the morning. And we started walking and it's hard walking already. And then my mother goes like that on my eyes and she doesn't want me to see. There were dead bodies on both sides of people [GESTURING] who went the previous days, and he, fell and froze. If they went like, had we gone like they did? And, my father wasn't athletic and my mother was holding me, and there was a man with us who was, from our part of Czechoslovakia. His name was, [THINKING] okay. I'm blanking. Okay. It'll come to me. [?Mezzi. Mezzi?] And he was, his wife was hiding somewhere else. And he was alone. And he was a stronger guy. So he asked my father, asked him if he could take me by the hand and pull me up because there were rocks. [GESTURING] It was there was nowhere to hold on. And if you fell, you fell to your death. So we had to kind of we made it up. And I actually have a description of that that was printed in the newspaper. I have it and I'll give it to you. And it's accounted by a lady who was older than me who made that crossing with her group. I actually met her at somebody's house here, and, she was with a bigger group. And she, when she was going, must have been a few days before us. They weren't sure. They thought that maybe somebody tricked them. And the other side is still Nazis. So they were afraid to go, but they started. And then they they had a couple with them who said, "We will go, we're not scared. And if, if there, if we don't come back in a certain time, take it as a signal that you don't come." Anyway, they came back. [GESTURING] And those people that this lady was with crossed properly, and we eventually crossed as well. And we come to the other side. It's liberated. The first thing they do with us, they're delousing stations because we were full of lice. So they took all your clothes, such as we had, and they put them through something to get rid of the lice. And then they washed us with, I don't know what. And I could feel the lice going from my hair and all over anyway. So now I was clean. I got the same yucky clothes back, all the layers put them on. And if you, I don't know if you, I mean a movie War and peace whatever. If you see devastation. So we start now to go back home. How do you go? Nothing's working. So you see this empty space with dead animals. There is a guy, a sick guy on a cart pulled by a horse. My father knows him. He's got typhus.

You know, I don't know how there was no transportation. My father finds a Russian convoy of trucks. And they're going east. They're going back east. So he gets a ride for us in the back of a truck. But it's like about ten trucks. And, progress was slow because their bridges were blown up and they said, "Everybody off." You have to help with the, my father was no builder of bridges. Anyway, somehow they got through. But at one of the stops, the guy, the driver or the guy who was with the driver of the foreign truck comes looking at what everything is doing, and he sees me and my parents in the back of a truck. So he said, you know, he'll take me and sit with him in the front so that that was okay. My parents allowed him. So that was interesting and I was more comfortable. And he comes to Humenné and he comes to the house with us. He was Jewish. He was a Jewish Soviet soldier. But he wouldn't, they were not to admit that that they were Jews. And he told me then, "You know, I have a little girl like you." and I could cry about that. He came back to see us again, so. But then they left. So that was the time when the Russians were our friends. And of course, there were many, many Jews in the army. So we come back. I don't know how many days that trip took. We come back to the house. Now you want to see who came back. Who is there. So, Uncle Alex, that was, we had two Alexes. [GESTURING]

INT: Come back to your home?

JS: To our home. So this was my grandparents home. They're not there. Of course we knew.

INT: Was it ransacked or?

JS: Yeah, it was. So the Russians were using it as one of their places. And like, we had wooden floors. So they made a bonfire in the middle of the floor. Things were like my some of the everything was strewn on the, you know, in the yard. [GESTURING] So I have some photographs were out in the yard on the ground and but nobody cared because we lost the people who cared about everything else. So and then you wait to see who turns up. So, my aunt, my mother's sister came back with a baby boy who was born just near the end of the war. So she called him Ivan. Yeah. [LAUGHING] He was a sweet little boy with a sad story in the end. And they come back. So now we are in the house together. There are no grandparents. And, Elizabeth, my aunt [UNCLEAR] So my father's sister hasn't turned up yet. She was in camp. Now, she was taken in '44 with her parents. And she comes to camp. And there are the young women, some of them from the families that my grandmother used to send food to in Humenné. Her mother. And they feed her, they help her survive. So she survived. And actually, these are the people who were written about in the 999. Some of them, they're from my hometown.

So she comes back, and she was actually engaged before the war. Her fiance was killed, and eventually she married somebody who was, you know, close to the family. And she goes to live in another town, and she has a baby boy, and. So mother and I and many people and mostly women, we, they come with food. The train passed not far. You know, it's a small town. [GESTURING] So the train is passing through and they go to the trains that come from the, from Poland. And when the train stops in Humenné, they give out the food, you know, because I'm sure there was no food on the train, people coming from camps and everybody is asking, "Did you see?" because you don't know who survived. So you're hoping you'll see somebody of your family who survived and there weren't many. So, I remember going to the trains and then as you start living, going to school. So schools were now public schools. I've never been in a public school before, and they're huge because they don't have the facilities. So they're like about 60 children in one grade. And I'm in first year gymnasium and, I remember now. In retrospect, sometimes I would sit under the desk and cry. And I, teacher wouldn't even notice that. And nobody told my mother, and I didn't tell my mother. There was nothing to tell. You know, it's just like we were still a group of Jewish children. So we were together somehow, and we would hear things like, look, more came back than left. And I have still the throwing stones at us. I had something here from a stone. [GESTURING]

INT: Who? Children?

JS: Children. Yeah. My, you know, classmates. But some were very nice. I mean, you know, that was in the soil there. You can't avoid it. So the Jewish kids kind of kept together, and then we started to be members of the Zionist group. And so we got some spirit that way. And we I actually went to a winter camp that my father really didn't want to send me, back in the Tatra mountains, you know, scary because another group of children were actually, you know, in danger. I think some were killed. There were still Jews killed after the war when they came back.

INT: By who?

JS: By the residents. Slovaks. There are plenty of Slovaks who were Nazis. You couldn't tell who was who. I forgot to mention. Or did I mention early on that one of the rules about Jews, they couldn't have any jewelry or any valuables? So you had to give it to the government. So they, one of the things they gave, they gave some things and some things they gave to neighbors to please, you know, candlesticks maybe. So when we came back, you asked the neighbors, they said, "You know, the Russians took it all.", which maybe they did. They didn't. But whatever. The government gave us back what we gave them. And one of the things that we gave them was a precious jewel piece of jewelry that I had. I was a little girl, and my Grossman grandfather gave me a tiny, tiny little star of David. Gold like that, and it was tiny. And they turned that in, you know, because you can't have gold.

JS: And they gave it back to me.

INT: Wow.

JS: They gave it back to me. So I have it in such a prized possession. That was my jewelry on my wedding day. My star of David. You have to look with a microscope to see it.

So. We now have Jewish groups together, and we go to, to public school. Gymnasium one, gymnasium two. I have a wonderful teacher, a Jewish teacher in gymnasium called Regina. Okay. Oh, God. [THINKING] [PAUSES] Deutsch. Regina Deutsch. And she has a little boy born after the war. He now lives in Toronto. He came. He came here after. When, you know, the communist regime ended. Sort of. When the borders opened or even before maybe he got out. His parents stayed in Slovakia. And I actually got to see them, and they came to visit him. And so that was nice. And we Jewish kids kind of stood out, but we stayed as a group and we had our Zionist group. And then really, once we realized that this is, nobody else is coming. The little girl, Aviva, of course, didn't come back. Her brother didn't. Her father didn't. Her mother did. And I remember her mother coming to the house, and I don't think she even had a photograph of her child. And then she just left. And I'm thinking, now, you know, nobody was tending to these people. She must have been so broken. I had no idea where she went. So it turned out she left for Palestine with her sister. She survived with her sister. And there I found out through this book. And then the person who was that she married and she had other children, and there was actually a movie made now in Israel by a young Israeli movie maker called Love It Was Not, And it tells the story of, [THINKING] of Aviva and Aviva's mother and the two children. So the father was separate. [GESTURING] They took the men separate. Killed him separately. Going into the gas chambers. And Aviva's aunt was one of the girls of the early transport. And she was there. She was extremely beautiful. Just absolute beauty. And one Nazi took a liking to her. Who knows what he did with her. And she could ask him to go to the gas chamber and get her sister out. Which he did. He took. He took Aviva's mother out and Aviva's mother turned back and Aviva apparently said to her, "Don't worry, I'll take care of the baby." The doors close. And she goes to her sister. The guy takes her to the sister, and the sister can't bear to tell her what's happened. So she she asked her and and the sister says, "No, they'll be fine. They're being looked after. You'll see them." Finally she finds out. I mean, I get shivers all through this, honestly. And this is what she survived. So an Israeli filmmaker made a movie about love it was not. The sister, actually. He was put on trial in Germany and the sister went to testify against him.

Now the sister and the mother have died, but the children got in touch with me because I made a note. [GESTURING] I commented when I heard about this that I knew Aviva and they didn't have the picture, that the child's name was Aviva and I named my daughter Aviva for her.

Yeah. So I'm. Anyway, I'm not much in touch with them. I find for some reason find it hard, but I've got to pick it up and do it. [PAUSES] So where are we now? We are now trying to gather ourselves, my father again at the helm. And no country wants Jews. There's Holocaust, you know. There are not many of us left.

So my father knew he wanted to get out. So now what? He tried Australia. He may have gotten something Venezuela, Canada, but we had relatives in the States. Nothing doing. My mother had. So my grandfather's brother and two brothers and a sister were in the States. But because we were like my father's other two brothers, [GESTURING] well, at least one brother who was not married to a Grossman. So the Grossman family couldn't sponsor everybody. And anyway, there was a long wait. You just couldn't do it. So, sorry. Before we're taking off. So different people land in Humenné to be with us. They're just the remnants of what happened to our family who were in Hungary. Hungary was really almost safe until it wasn't. And then it got brutal. So, my aunt Rose, who was married to my grandfather, Grossman's youngest brother, they were, they went to Budapest. They lived in Kosice, but then they went to Budapest for some reason. And she has Otto, and Alex. And she's pregnant and her husband is with her. And one day her husband walks out and he goes, I guess, to get some food. And he takes Alex with him and they both disappear. Gone. So now she is looking after her children and she has to get food and not be rounded up. And she pretends that she's. Deaf and dumb that she can't. If she's asked questions, she's deaf. She can't hear. I don't know how she got through that. She was a very beautiful woman. And she survives the war. And Susie, her baby girl, is born right after the war. And now she can't manage because she's got all these kids and she has nothing. So the kids get doled out. She keeps Michael and the baby with her. [?Erwin?] comes to live with us in Humenné, and he goes to school with me, and I'm so thrilled. He's three years older than me. I have an older brother. Otto goes to Bratislava to be with Maja's family. And Rosa's husband's brother. So my grandfather's brother, who became a very prominent successful man in New York, he now works on getting them to America. And they will leave shortly. But in the meantime, we've got [?Erwin?] there. Then there was a, there were two twins, a boy and a girl who were related to my father, and they were from Hungary. Everybody in the family gone. They were in camp. I don't know what experiments were done on them. He was a tall, strong looking guy. She was a petite girl. They were twins. They came to stay with us. And an aunt also who has nobody left. So they come to Humenné and we have a house full.

JS: It was wonderful and so--

INT: Were people sad? Were, did they tell their stories?

JS: No. Nobody told stories. No. You just, no, no stories. I don't remember stories. So, eventually, so Irwin is eventually brought to America by my grandfather's brother, Isadore. [?Erwin?] and aunt Rose and his siblings. They come to America, and they prosper because they all go to university. They're all brilliant and very successful. [GESTURING]

There is an interesting, a cousin. So the uncle, my grandfather's Grossman's brother Maurice and his wife Sarah were childless. And they took, I don't know how they worked it Another brother of my grandfather, another sister of my grandfather's, had four children. And the the childless couple took in

So [?Lily?] survived through horrible things, but she survived with her adoptive parents. By her aunt and uncle. [GESTURING] And her mother, father and siblings were all killed. She survived horrific like, she really, I have a story somewhere and I don't have the details. She survives. Uncle Isidore again. The great Uncle Isidore brings her to America with her father, uncle, Uncle Maurice [UNCLEAR] and she works at Isidore's factory. Had a men's clothing factory. Groschen clothes for Grossman. It was on Fifth Avenue. He had a massive apartment on Fifth Avenue. You know, he's really. And he makes now, so they all managed because of his help. And he, we're, so I didn't, I'm going back and forth. We're still in Slovakia, so coming back, we're, [THINKING] We are Humenné and there is no place that will take us. And my father is desperate to get us all out because this Iron Curtain is coming, you know. It's now '45, '46, '47. We are still there. You're not allowed to sell your house. You cannot exchange your money to dollars, or you go to jail. You can't, you, in other words, you can take your clothes and go, but just some clothes. And so my father manages on the black market to get some dollars. And then we are still in Humenné. And he gets word that they're going to come and search the house. So I am now, what, 11 years old. And I have a school bag like this school bag going to school. And he puts the dollars in my school bag and he says, "Okay, sweetheart, you go to school." And I go to school, I know what I have. And they didn't come searching. So now he still has the dollars. He's got to figure out how to transmit them. So my travel, my father travels to Prague all the time and he tries to get into the embassies to befriends somebody who would help him. So he finds a guy in the French embassy who will take some money for him. So he transfers some dollars to him and you know, it goes to, and something else, I don't know. And it goes to France with him because they have immunity. And, then he found out that he could get, if he got a visitor's visa to Canada. Canada still doesn't want Jews. This is '48. Visitor's visa to Canada. And he comes here and he becomes a farmer. He'll become a landed immigrant. So he proceeds for the paperwork. He gets himself this visitor's visa. For my mother and me only for France. We only have visas for France. And we all leave for France. So now, this is not an easy process, because you're not allowed to sell anything and you can't bring too much with you. So you have to make a list of stuff that you'll take, for instance, two blouses, not three. They'll, you submit the list, stupid things, and then they'll say, no, no, you can't have three. You can just have two. Then they send somebody from the government and they'll do the packing. According to what's on the sheet that's allowed. And I had a friend who played the mandolin, and I told father that she put it on her list and she was allowed a mandolin. I didn't play anything. My father went out and bought a mandolin. [LAUGHING] At least he'll have mandolin instead of the money that he has to leave behind something. So we come with the mandolin and we packed by the official people, and we take the train to, through Czechoslovakia to Prague and pass to the German border. My two uncles come with us, but when we get to the border, they have to get off the train and they stand behind the fence. My father's two brothers, and they keep saying, "Don't forget about us." And my mother and I and my father get on the train. And at that point, the guys that duty or whoever takes us off the train because we have to be searched. So they take us to separate rooms. I'm with my mother. I think I had long braids, they undo my hair to see I don't have anything on my hair. I had to take all my clothes off and we didn't have anything on us because you wouldn't be stupid enough to do it. But I remember the moment when we got back on the train because we didn't. I wasn't even together with my father when they finally let us back on the train. Then the train started moving. I thought it was just such a good feeling, but I looked back and my two uncles are waving and say, "Don't forget about us." [GESTURING] because he they didn't have permit to go yet with their families. So by now they each have a wife and a baby boy. So we arrive in Paris and we rent a little room up in the top floor. [GESTURING] There was nothing there. There's no washroom. There's a bidet in the room, you know, main thing in France. [GESTURING] And my father stays a little while with us. And then he takes off for Canada. And he's met on the dock by his cousin from Youngstown, Ohio, whose parents are the ones that my parents wanted to send me to in Hungary to keep me safe. They were all wiped out, his sister, their family, and his name was And he waited on the dock for my father. That was wonderful. And my father started to get, get going. At that time, the government of Ontario, of Canada wanted farmers so they, he could connect. And he had a friend who was from Slovakia who was a lawyer, and he spoke English already. So he helped my father and they sent two people from Ottawa with a car to take my father to buy a farm. So my father always regretted that they didn't take him to Finch and Bathurst. He went to Blythe, Ontario and outside Blythe, not in Blythe. He buys a 220 acre farm for 12,500 dollars.

